---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/seo/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EmbedChart from '../components/seo/EmbedChart.astro';
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../utils/schema';
import { getCanonicalUrl, SITE } from '../utils/seo';
import babyData from '../data/baby-stool-colors.json';
import charts from '../data/chart-images.json';

const url = getCanonicalUrl(Astro.url.pathname);
const title = 'Baby Poop Color Chart: What Every Diaper Color Means';
const description =
  'A baby poop color chart for every stage — meconium, breastfed, formula, and solids. What each color means, and the three that need a doctor the same day.';

const CHART = charts.charts['baby-poop-color-chart'];
const PRINT = charts.charts['baby-poop-color-chart-printable'];
const ALT =
  'Baby poop color chart showing nine diaper colors — black meconium, dark green transitional, mustard yellow, tan, bright green, brown, white, red and black — with what each means and which need a doctor.';

const flags: Record<string, { label: string; color: string }> = {
  normal: { label: 'Normal', color: 'var(--color-primary)' },
  watch: { label: 'Usually fine', color: '#FFB86B' },
  urgent: { label: 'Call a doctor', color: '#FF6B6B' },
};

const urgent = babyData.colors.filter((c: any) => c.flag === 'urgent');

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What color should baby poop be?',
    answer:
      'It depends on age and feeding. The first few days bring black, tar-like meconium, which is normal. Breastfed babies then settle into loose mustard-yellow, seedy stool. Formula-fed babies produce thicker tan to yellow-brown stool. Once solids start, it darkens to brown. All of these are normal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What baby poop colors are dangerous?',
    answer:
      'Three. White, pale or chalky stool can mean bile is not reaching the gut and is the key early sign of biliary atresia — contact a doctor the same day. Red stool or blood streaks always warrant a call, even though the cause is often a minor anal fissure or a milk protein allergy. Black, tarry stool after meconium has cleared, in a baby not taking iron, can be digested blood.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is green baby poop normal?',
    answer:
      'Usually, yes. Green stool is commonly caused by iron-fortified formula, a foremilk-heavy feed, something in a breastfeeding parent’s diet, or a mild viral illness. If the baby is feeding well, gaining weight and generally content, green alone is not a concern. Green combined with poor feeding, fever, or unusual lethargy is worth a call.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is my newborn’s poop black?',
    answer:
      'In the first 24 to 48 hours, black is meconium — the sticky, tar-like material the gut accumulated before birth, and entirely normal. It should clear within two to three days. Black stool appearing after meconium has passed is different, and in a baby not on iron supplements it should be treated as urgent.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often should a baby poop?',
    answer:
      'The range is very wide. A breastfed newborn may pass stool at nearly every feed, while an older breastfed baby can go several days between movements and still be perfectly healthy. Formula-fed babies are typically less frequent than breastfed newborns. Consistency and comfort matter more than frequency — a soft stool passed without distress is fine, however often it arrives.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  articleSchema({
    title,
    description,
    url,
    image: CHART.src,
    imageAlt: ALT,
    images: [{ url: PRINT.src, width: PRINT.width, height: PRINT.height, caption: `${ALT} Printable version.` }],
    datePublished: '2026-08-12T00:00:00Z',
    dateModified: '2026-08-12T00:00:00Z',
    author: 'PoopCheck Team',
    medical: true,
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITE.url}/` },
    { name: 'Baby Poop Color Chart', url },
  ]),
  faqSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<PageLayout seo={{ title, description, type: 'article', image: CHART.src, imageAlt: ALT }} schema={schema}>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Baby Poop Color Chart', href: '/baby-poop-color-chart/' }]} />
      </div>

      <header class="mb-8 text-center">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight">
          Baby Poop Color Chart
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Newborn stool changes dramatically in the first week, then again with
          formula and again with solids. Almost all of it is normal. Here is what
          each color means — and the three that need a doctor the same day.
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* The urgent cases go above the fold. A worried parent should not have to scroll. */}
      <div class="mb-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#FF6B6B]/35 bg-[#FF6B6B]/[0.07] p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3 text-[#FF6B6B]">Call a doctor today if you see these</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2">
          {urgent.map((c: any) => (
            <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span
                class="w-5 h-5 rounded-md shrink-0 mt-0.5 border border-white/15"
                style={`background-color: ${c.hex};`}
                aria-hidden="true"
              ></span>
              <span class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">{c.name}</strong> — {c.meaning}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Photograph the diaper in natural light before you change it — color is hard
          to describe over the phone and easy to show.
        </p>
      </div>

      <EmbedChart
        src={CHART.src}
        alt={ALT}
        width={CHART.width}
        height={CHART.height}
        title="Baby Poop Color Chart"
        caption="Baby poop color chart: every diaper color from meconium through solids, with what each one means."
        creditPath="/baby-poop-color-chart/"
        eager={true}
        printable={{ src: PRINT.src, width: PRINT.width, height: PRINT.height }}
      />

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6 mt-16">Every diaper color, explained</h2>

      <div class="space-y-5">
        {babyData.colors.map((c: any) => {
          const flag = flags[c.flag];
          return (
            <div
              id={c.slug}
              class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--color-border)]/50 bg-[var(--color-surface)] p-6 scroll-mt-24"
            >
              <div class="flex items-start gap-4 mb-3 flex-wrap">
                <div
                  class="w-14 h-14 rounded-xl shrink-0 border border-white/10"
                  style={`background-color: ${c.hex};`}
                  aria-hidden="true"
                ></div>
                <div class="flex-1 min-w-[200px]">
                  <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">{c.name}</h3>
                  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">
                    <em>{c.when}</em> — {c.meaning}
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div
                  class="text-xs font-semibold px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full self-center"
                  style={`background-color: color-mix(in oklab, ${flag.color} 15%, transparent); color: ${flag.color}; border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, ${flag.color} 30%, transparent);`}
                >
                  {flag.label}
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">{c.detail}</p>
            </div>
          );
        })}
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-invert prose-lg max-w-none mt-16
        prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:text-[var(--color-text)]
        prose-h2:text-2xl prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h2:mb-4
        prose-h3:text-xl prose-h3:mt-8 prose-h3:mb-3
        prose-p:text-[var(--color-text-muted)] prose-p:leading-relaxed
        prose-a:text-[var(--color-accent)] prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
        prose-strong:text-[var(--color-text)]
        prose-li:text-[var(--color-text-muted)]
      ">
        <h2>What changes, and when</h2>
        <p>
          The first week is the most dramatic. Meconium clears in two to three days,
          transitional stool follows, and by day four or five a feeding pattern
          establishes itself. Breastfed and formula-fed stool then look genuinely
          different from each other, and neither is a problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          Solids change everything again — thicker, browner, considerably smellier,
          and often containing recognizable pieces of food. That is normal digestion,
          not a failure of it.
        </p>

        <h2>Frequency matters less than you think</h2>
        <p>
          A breastfed newborn might fill a diaper at nearly every feed. An older
          breastfed baby might go several days without one and be entirely well.
          What matters is that the stool is soft and passed without distress. A hard,
          pellet-like stool that clearly hurts is worth raising even if it arrives
          daily.
        </p>

        <p><em>
          This page summarizes mainstream pediatric guidance and is not medical advice
          or a diagnosis. Trust your instincts — if something seems wrong with your
          baby, contact your pediatrician regardless of what a chart says.
        </em></p>

        <h2>FAQ</h2>
        {faqItems.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.question}</h3>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </>
        ))}

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul>
          {babyData.sources.map((s: any) => (
            <li><a href={s.url} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{s.label}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        <h2>Related</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/poop-color-chart/">Poop color chart for adults</a></li>
          <li><a href="/bristol/">The Bristol Stool Chart</a></li>
          <li><a href="/poopcheck-blog/how-green-stool-in-infants-predicts-future-bowel-health-insights/">Green stool in infants</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
